<clickhouse>
    <!-- Bounds ClickHouse's OWN observability, which the stock image leaves
         unbounded. Measured on the mainnet deployment 2026-07-30, 15 days after
         go-live: the ClickHouse volume was 32 GB, of which ~30 GB was these
         system log tables and ~1 GB was actual TMS data. system.text_log alone
         held 14.2 GB / 380 M rows because the stock image runs it at
         <level>trace</level>. Eight of the nine tables configured below ship
         with NO ttl and grow forever; only processors_profile_log carries one
         (30 days). Combined that is ~2.0 GiB/day at mainnet write rates, versus
         ~62 MiB/day for the detection data itself: about thirty to one.

         Verified against clickhouse/clickhouse-server:26.1.3, the tag
         docker-compose.yml pins. The stock config defines 23 log tables but
         only the ones this workload actually creates matter, so the audit that
         matters is "which EXISTING table has no TTL", not "which table exists":
           SELECT name, if(position(engine_full,'TTL')>0,'has TTL','NO TTL')
           FROM system.tables WHERE database='system' AND name LIKE '%log%'
         Run it after an image bump or a feature change. Anything reported
         NO TTL grows forever and belongs in the list below. error_log and
         background_schedule_pool_log were added on 2026-08-03 by exactly this
         check, having started being written after the first pass assumed they
         would stay empty.

         NOTE for EXISTING deployments: these settings apply when ClickHouse
         CREATES a log table, so mounting this file reclaims nothing by itself.
         A table that already exists with a different TTL is not migrated in
         place; on restart ClickHouse renames it to <name>_0 and creates a fresh
         empty one with the TTL below. All nine are renamed here, because every
         stock TTL differs from 7 days, and the renamed tables hold all the old
         data with no TTL of their own. Reclaiming space therefore means
         DROP TABLE system.<name>_0, not TRUNCATE of the new tables. RUNBOOK.md
         ("ClickHouse disk use") has the full procedure, including the in-place
         alternative and why TRUNCATE must precede MODIFY TTL there (the ALTER
         materializes the TTL across every existing part, which on a 14 GB table
         exceeded the container's 4 GB mem_limit mid-mutation). -->

    <!-- Server log FILES (the clickhouse_logs volume), separate from the log
         tables above. Stock default is level=trace at 1000M x 10, i.e. up to
         ~10 GB.

         The level and the cap have to move TOGETHER. Cutting the cap 33x while
         leaving the level at 'trace' would hold only hours of history at
         mainnet write rates, which contradicts the 7-day table TTL below: an
         operator triaging Monday morning would find the tables intact and the
         server logs already rotated away. At 'information' the same 300 MB
         spans days, so both halves agree on the triage window.

         Note this is the FILE logger. The <level> inside <text_log> below is a
         separate setting governing that table only. -->
    <logger>
        <level>information</level>
        <size>100M</size>
        <count>3</count>
    </logger>

    <!-- 7 days is the incident-triage window for this stack: long enough to
         investigate a weekend outage on the following Monday, short enough to
         bound the volume.

         Sizes at 7-day steady state, measured on mainnet 2026-08-03 with this
         file live, rather than estimated. They differ per table by three orders
         of magnitude, so a single "about a GB each" figure (which this comment
         used to give) is wrong in both directions:

           query_log   ~1.56 GiB   838 K rows/day  @ 286.1 B/row
           trace_log   ~750 MiB    see the profiler note below
           text_log    ~2.8 MiB    8.6 K rows/day  @  46.6 B/row
           others      ~120 MiB    asynchronous_metric_log dominates the rest

         Total lands near 2.4 GiB against the ~30 GiB per 15 days the stock
         image reached unbounded. -->
    <text_log>
        <!-- The single highest-value line in this file. At stock 'trace' this
             table took 22.3 M rows/day (1.04 GiB/day, ~7.3 GiB at a 7-day TTL)
             and was the largest object in the volume. Measured after the switch
             to 'information': 6 rows/minute, zero Trace and zero Debug, i.e.
             ~2,500x less, for ~2.8 MiB at steady state. 'information' still
             keeps startup, DDL, warning and error context, which is what
             triage actually reads. -->
        <level>information</level>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </text_log>
    <query_log>
        <!-- Largest remaining table once the two above are bounded, and worth
             it: this is the table that answers "what ran, when, how long".
             77.5% of its rows are queries under 10 ms, so
             log_queries_min_query_duration_ms would cut it to ~350 MiB. Left
             unset deliberately: that also erases the volume signal, and a flood
             of fast queries is a real failure mode worth being able to see. -->
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_log>
    <trace_log>
        <!-- Sampling profiler output, not diagnostics. Measured composition
             before the profiler was disabled (see clickhouse/users.d/
             query-profiler.xml): Real 59.2%, Memory 20.8%, MemoryPeak 19.7%,
             CPU 0.2%. The Real/CPU half is query profiling and had never been
             read in this deployment, so it is off. The Memory half is retained
             on purpose: this container runs under a 4 GiB mem_limit it has
             already hit mid-mutation, and memory traces are what explain that.
             Hence ~750 MiB rather than the ~1.85 GiB it was heading for. -->
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </trace_log>
    <!-- This one reached 7.19 GiB / 271.3 M rows in 15 days on mainnet despite
         already carrying a TTL: it is the ONLY one of the nine with a stock TTL,
         verified as 30 days on 26.1.3. At its measured 486 MiB/day, pinning it
         to the same 7 days as the rest should hold it near 3.3 GiB. -->
    <processors_profile_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </processors_profile_log>
    <part_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </part_log>
    <metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </metric_log>
    <asynchronous_metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </asynchronous_metric_log>
    <query_views_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_views_log>
    <query_metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_metric_log>
    <!-- Added 2026-08-03. The first pass assumed these two stayed empty on this
         workload and left them alone; the audit query above found them being
         written, at 55 and 77 rows. Harmless at that size, and that is the
         point: without a TTL they only ever grow, which is the whole reason
         this file exists. Covering them now costs nothing and removes the need
         to notice later. -->
    <error_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </error_log>
    <background_schedule_pool_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </background_schedule_pool_log>
</clickhouse>
